```apex
/**
 * Sends renewal-due reminder emails to opportunity owners.
 *
 * Called weekly by a scheduled job with up to 200 opportunity Ids.
 *
 * Design notes / corrections to the original approach:
 *
 * 1. LIMITS: Messaging.sendEmail counts against the "Email Invocations" limit,
 *    which is 10 per transaction. Calling sendEmail once per opportunity in a
 *    loop over up to 200 records would hit call #11, throw an uncatchable
 *    System.LimitException, and abort the whole run. We therefore send emails
 *    in chunks of at most 10 messages per sendEmail call. Failures are still
 *    isolated at chunk granularity: a rejection of one message (invalid Id,
 *    inactive user, etc.) does not affect other messages or other chunks, and
 *    we count successes per message from SendEmailResult.
 *    If per-opportunity isolation is truly required, the scheduled job must
 *    split its work so no transaction gets more than 10 Ids (e.g. via a
 *    batch job or System.scheduleBatch) — an Schedulable passing 200 Ids at
 *    once cannot satisfy that with one call per record.
 *
 * 2. SINGLE SOQL: owner names/emails and opportunity data are fetched in one
 *    query for all Ids; no per-record queries or DML.
 *
 * 3. NO ALLOCATION USAGE: emails are addressed to Users by Id with
 *    setSaveAsActivity(false), so they do not consume the org's daily
 *    external-email allocation. setSaveAsActivity(true) is also illegal for
 *    user-targeted emails, so false is mandatory here anyway.
 *
 * 4. TESTABILITY: email dispatch goes through an injectable EmailSender
 *    interface so unit tests never invoke real email (which would otherwise
 *    be sent as the running user in tests and count against limits).
 */
public inherited sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /** Apex "sendEmail" counts against this per-transaction limit. */
    @TestVisible
    private static final Integer MAX_EMAIL_INVOCATIONS_PER_TX = 10;

    /** Injectable seam for email dispatch. */
    public interface EmailSender {
        List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> send(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages);
    }

    /** Real dispatcher used in production. */
    public with sharing class ApexEmailSender implements EmailSender {
        public List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> send(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages) {
            return Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false); // false = do not stop on first error
        }
    }

    @TestVisible
    private static EmailSender sender = new ApexEmailSender();

    /**
     * Emails the owner of each given opportunity about the upcoming renewal.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds up to 200 opportunity Ids; null/duplicate/unknown Ids are skipped
     * @return number of reminder emails that were sent successfully
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // De-duplicate and drop nulls; keep a stable order for predictability.
        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id oppId : opportunityIds) {
            if (oppId != null) {
                ids.add(oppId);
            }
        }
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // One SOQL for all opportunities and their owners.
        // OwnerId may point to a User (standard case) or a Queue (Opportunity is
        // one of the objects that allows queue ownership); only Users can be
        // emailed via setTargetObjectId, so we filter with owner.isActive = true.
        Map<Id, Opportunity> oppsById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>([
            SELECT Id,
                   Name,
                   CloseDate,
                   OwnerId,
                   Owner.IsActive
            FROM   Opportunity
            WHERE  Id IN :ids
            AND    Owner.IsActive = true
        ]);

        Integer sent = 0;
        List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> chunk =
            new List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>(MAX_EMAIL_INVOCATIONS_PER_TX);

        for (Opportunity opp : oppsById.values()) {
            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage mail = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            mail.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);          // User Id: no daily allocation used
            mail.setSaveAsActivity(false);                 // required for user-targeted emails
            mail.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            mail.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,\n\n'
                + 'The renewal opportunity "' + opp.Name + '" is due soon.\n'
                + 'Close date: ' + opp.CloseDate.format() + '\n\n'
                + 'Please review it and take the necessary actions.\n'
            );

            chunk.add(mail);

            if (chunk.size() == MAX_EMAIL_INVOCATIONS_PER_TX) {
                sent += dispatch(chunk);
                chunk.clear();
            }
        }
        if (!chunk.isEmpty()) {
            sent += dispatch(chunk);
        }

        return sent;
    }

    /** Sends one chunk and counts the messages that succeeded. */
    private static Integer dispatch(List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage> messages) {
        Integer successes = 0;
        try {
            List<Messaging.SendEmailResult> results = sender.send(messages);
            for (Messaging.SendEmailResult result : results) {
                if (result != null && result.isSuccess()) {
                    successes++;
                }
                // Per-message errors are deliberately not re-thrown: the goal is
                // to deliver as many reminders as possible and report the count.
            }
        } catch (System.EmailException | System.NoAccessException e) {
            // Whole chunk rejected (e.g. limit breach, org-wide email off).
            // Swallow so remaining chunks still get a chance to go out.
            System.debug(LoggingLevel.ERROR, 'RenewalReminderService email dispatch failed: ' + e);
        }
        return successes;
    }
}
```

# Solution

## Why I changed the approach: the "one query" plan doesn't survive contact with 2.3M contacts

The core business logic in your spec is exactly right: sync `MailingCountry` only when `BillingCountry` changes, and only touch contacts whose `MailingCountry` is blank or equals the old country. But the implementation plan — "load *all* contacts once into a map" — is unworkable at your data volume, so I fixed that part.

**1. Querying 2.3M contacts violates the query row limit and is unnecessary.**
`[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]` at 2.3M rows will throw `System.LimitException: Too many query rows` (50,000 limit) on the very first run — with MDM batches of 200 accounts, the trigger context is almost certainly also running inside larger Batch Apex/Data Loader (Bulk API) transactions where the limit applies per transaction, so there's no batching of the trigger that saves you. It's also massive over-fetching: you only ever need contacts of the *200 accounts in this batch* whose `MailingCountry` is blank or equal to the old country — typically a few hundred rows, not millions.

**2. "No queries in loops" ≠ "no queries".** The real bulk-safe pattern is *one query outside the loop, filtered to what the batch actually needs*. That's what I implemented:

- Build two sets: the `AccountIds` in the batch, and — for the filter — the old countries per account (collected into a map *before* any query, so no SOQL inside a loop).
- Run **a single SOQL** for the whole batch: contacts of these accounts where `MailingCountry = null OR MailingCountry IN :oldCountries`. Adding `MailingCountry IN` to the WHERE clause is also far more selective (and index-friendly) than post-filtering in Apex, and I re-check the condition in Apex anyway so correctness never depends on the query.
- Aggregate the results into a `Map<Id, List<Contact>>` in one loop, then update all candidate contacts in **one DML** outside the loop.

**3. Old value in an after-update trigger.** In `Trigger.new`/`Trigger.old`, `Trigger.old` holds the pre-update values, so the "previous BillingCountry" comes from the matching `Trigger.oldMap` record. (If you ever move this to a `before update`-fed service or an async context, `Trigger.old` may not be available — then `Account.BillingCountry`'s history would be the fallback; not needed here.)

**4. Also bulk-safe on the other limits:** the candidate lists stay far under 10,000 DML rows for realistic MDM batches (200 accounts → a handful of contacts each); one DML call total; collections are the only state. No workflow/recursive-trigger loops since we only touch `Contact` from the `Account` trigger — but I still guard against recursion in case a contact trigger ever writes back to Account.

